### Description

A weird interaction breaks inheritance when using ``@validate_call`` with a method that is implemented on both a ``Parent`` class and ``Child`` class that inherits from ``Parent``, with the ``Child`` class method calling the ``Parent`` method via  ``super()``.

As per my code example, the first time a ``Child`` object calls the method ``do_something``, everything works fine, but the second time, the ``Child.do_something`` method has been overwritten by the ``Parent.do_something``method. I've tried few combinations:
* If I remove ``@validate_call`` and keep ``super().do_something`` in the child method, things work properly (pure python, works expected);
* If I keep ``@validate_call`` and remove ``super().do_something`` in the child method, things work properly;
* If I keep ``@validate_call`` and replace ``super().do_something`` by ``Parent.do_something(self, ...)`` in the child method, things work properly;

I believe that the ``super().do_something(a=a, b=b)`` call within the ``Child.do_something`` method triggers some validation that binds the ``do_something`` method of the ``Parrent`` class to the ``Child`` class.


